[s0l0!-header][h1]Arguments[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Arguments[/b] are a way for our functions to take input parameters.

For example, we can add a [b]name[/b] argument to our [b]welcome[/b]() function and use it in the output:[code format="go" ]func welcome([b]name string[/b]) {
  fmt.Println("Hello, "+name)
} [/code]
As you can see, the argument is provided in the parentheses and includes the name followed by the type.

The argument behaves like a variable inside the function's body, meaning you can use its value by its name.

When calling the function, we need to pass it a string argument inside the parentheses:[code format="go" codeId="3322"]func main() {
  welcome("David")
  welcome("James")
} [/code]
We are able to reuse the code in our function by calling it with different arguments.
[note]Remember, when defining the function, the type of the argument comes after the variable name.[/note]

